To connect to a remote MCP server over HTTP and use its tools with `streamText`, follow these steps:

1. Create an MCP client with HTTP transport
2. Retrieve the available tools
3. Pass the tools to `streamText`

Here's a concise example:

```ts
import { createMCPClient } from '@ai-sdk/mcp';
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

async function callRemoteMCPServer() {
  // Create an MCP client connected to a remote HTTP server
  const client = await createMCPClient({
    transport: {
      type: 'http',
      url: 'http://your-remote-mcp-server.com/mcp',
      // Optional: add headers for authentication
      // headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer your-token' },
    },
  });

  try {
    // Get all available tools from the MCP server
    const tools = await client.tools();

    // Use the tools with streamText
    const response = await streamText({
      model: openai('gpt-4o'),
      tools,
      prompt: 'Use the remote MCP tools to help me...',
      onEnd: async () => {
        await client.close(); // Clean up when done
      },
      onError: async (error) => {
        await client.close(); // Clean up on error
      },
    });

    // Stream the response
    for await (const chunk of response.textStream) {
      console.log(chunk);
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error:', error);
    await client.close();
  }
}
```

For production, use the HTTP transport as shown above [7]. The client will automatically handle the connection to your remote MCP server, and you can pass the retrieved tools directly to `streamText` [2].

Yes. You can enable automatic retries for transient MCP tool failures (such as rate limits or gateway timeouts) by passing the `maxRetries` option when you create the client [1]:

```typescript
const mcpClient = await createMCPClient({
  transport: {
    type: 'http',
    url: 'https://your-server.com/mcp',
  },
  maxRetries: 2, // enables automatic retries
});
```

Retries are disabled by default and only apply to `tools/call` requests for transient HTTP/network failures; JSON-RPC application errors are not retried [1].

